    "turns": [
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Why does Ridge deserve its own place in the history of artificial intelligence?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "editorial-hook"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Arthur Hoerl and Robert Kennard introduced ridge regression as a biased estimation method for unstable least-squares problems with nonorthogonal predictors. Adding a small positive value to the diagonal of the normal equations made coefficient estimates less variable.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-ridge-regression-biased-estimation-for-nonorthogonal-problems"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.historical-background"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "A penalty term shrinks coefficients so correlated predictors no longer dominate the fit.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-ridge-regression-biased-estimation-for-nonorthogonal-problems"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.core-idea"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Experts generally treat ridge regression as a classic regularization result that made controlled bias a practical tool. Its long-term legacy is the L2 penalty pattern used across statistics, kernel methods, and deep-learning weight decay.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-ridge-regression-biased-estimation-for-nonorthogonal-problems"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.long-term-legacy"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "As today's systems grow larger, which lessons from Ridge still matter, and which limits deserve to be questioned again?",
